############################################
# using CD HIT to cluster the seqs 
###
## NOTE 1: all the genes seq were checked and 98% clustering identity seems to be suitable since it is keeps the species info/resolution for each of the genes of interest, look at the excel file clust_thres_genes_of_interest
## NOTE 2: I catted all the seqs for all genes of interest into one file and then chceked their percentage identity across all the 469 seqs. Seems like they all are more than 98% diff so we can stick to the 98% clustering threshold. Except for three very short seqs  has similarity with the other segs. one of them torA is very short compared to the other torA seqs can so could be non-specific, so using a size threshold, say the sequence needs to be at least 70% length of the cluster representative. This should work for all cases, but for the norC seqs that are similar to the A. guillouiae napA seqs we need to see if we look norC represemntataion is lost from the applying a clustering size threshold --_ WE DID GET REP SEQS FOR norC with -c 0.98 and -s 0.7, SO ALL GOOD HERE
###

# running CDHIT now 
# cd into the folder
#cd all_genes_fasta_renamed
#module load cd-hit/4.8.1
#mkdir -p cd_hit_output
#cd-hit -i all_gene_seqs.fasta -o cd_hit_output/cd_hit_output.fasta -c 0.98 -s 0.7 -n 5 -T 8 -sc 0
# -c 0.98 - 98% clustering threshold 
# -s 0.7 - seqs need to be at least 70% of the cluster rep to be included in the rep seq
# -n 5 - word size of 5 is recommended fir clustering threshold of 0.7 to 1.0 (refer https://github.com/weizhongli/cdhit/blob/master/doc/cdhit-user-guide.wiki)
# -sc 0 - defualt is 0 so technically no need to specify, but this sorts cluster rep seqs in decreasing order of length
#module unload cd-hit/4.8.1

